WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:02.235 line:0%
-Thanks for coming on.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:04.236 line:0%
I just saw your beautiful
wife backstage.

00:00:04.236 --> 00:00:05.905 line:0%
And today is the 20th
anniversary

00:00:05.905 --> 00:00:06.906 line:0%
of you guys meeting.

00:00:06.906 --> 00:00:08.507 line:0%
-Yeah.
This is the day we met.

00:00:08.507 --> 00:00:10.442
20 years ago.
-Isn't that cool?

00:00:10.442 --> 00:00:12.044
-Yeah.
-That's awesome, buddy.

00:00:12.044 --> 00:00:14.580
-Yeah, it's really weird,
actually.

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.815
And the movie "Air"
is opening today,

00:00:16.815 --> 00:00:19.685
and we realize that
Michael Jordan wore two numbers

00:00:19.685 --> 00:00:24.890
in his career, 45 and 23,
and the date is 4-5-23.

00:00:24.890 --> 00:00:26.258
-Holy!
-Isn't it crazy?

00:00:26.258 --> 00:00:28.595
-Yes. Come on. That's fun.

00:00:28.595 --> 00:00:29.996
-But much more importantly,

00:00:29.996 --> 00:00:31.997
it's 20 years
since I met Lucy, so...

00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:33.800
-Of course. Yeah. Yeah,
that's much more important.

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:35.568
How is the fam?
Last time we talked,

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:37.704
you were moving everyone
from L.A. to New York.

00:00:37.704 --> 00:00:39.038
-Right. Yeah, we did. We --

00:00:39.038 --> 00:00:41.241
It was the smartest thing
we ever did, we're loving it.

00:00:41.241 --> 00:00:42.976
-You're here?
-Yeah. We're in Brooklyn.

00:00:42.976 --> 00:00:44.610
-Yeah!
-Very, very happy. Yeah, yeah.

00:00:44.610 --> 00:00:47.446
-You're loving it.
-Loving it. Loving it.

00:00:47.446 --> 00:00:49.115
-Let's talk about
the new movie "Air."

00:00:49.115 --> 00:00:51.184
I got to see it.
And the reviews are in.

00:00:51.184 --> 00:00:53.386
Everyone loves it.
It is fantastic, buddy.

00:00:53.386 --> 00:00:54.587
You're so good in this.

00:00:54.587 --> 00:00:55.888
Everyone's good in it,
by the way.

00:00:55.888 --> 00:00:56.989
-Everybody's great.

00:00:56.989 --> 00:00:59.359
I mean, we got just
a lights out cast.

00:00:59.359 --> 00:01:01.060
-You really do.
But everything was good.

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.430
The script was tight.
You and Ben Affleck, obviously,

00:01:04.430 --> 00:01:05.665
everyone loves
you guys together.

00:01:05.665 --> 00:01:09.335
You're great.
This was 25 years ago.

00:01:09.335 --> 00:01:10.536
-Oh, yeah.
-Look at this.

00:01:10.536 --> 00:01:15.041
When you won the Oscars
for "Good Will Hunting."

00:01:15.041 --> 00:01:16.676
Look at how happy!

00:01:16.676 --> 00:01:18.744
"Yeah!"

00:01:18.744 --> 00:01:22.348
-We don't look too excited.
-Yeah. I mean, you got to be --

00:01:22.348 --> 00:01:23.716
I mean, what's going
through your --

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:25.785
Remember this? Were you pumped?
-I do. Well, we didn't --

00:01:25.785 --> 00:01:28.121
The weird thing about that
was that we, like,

00:01:28.121 --> 00:01:30.356
the year before we were
watching it on TV,

00:01:30.356 --> 00:01:32.024
it was like -- we had no --

00:01:32.024 --> 00:01:34.027
there was no adjustment period

00:01:34.027 --> 00:01:35.394
where we went, like,
we just suddenly

00:01:35.394 --> 00:01:37.096
were kind of there
in the front row

00:01:37.096 --> 00:01:39.499
and Billy Crystal sang a song
with our names in it.

00:01:39.499 --> 00:01:40.700
We were like, What is happening?

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.067
-What is going on with
our lives?

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:45.071
-Yeah. And we hadn't --
we hadn't talked about

00:01:45.071 --> 00:01:46.639
like a speech
or anything like that

00:01:46.639 --> 00:01:50.309
because I think, as kind
of lifelong Red Sox fans,

00:01:50.309 --> 00:01:52.011
we, you know, you don't
want to jinx anything.

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:54.546
-Yeah, exactly.
-Because I think both of us,

00:01:54.546 --> 00:01:56.249
without even talking about it,
we were like...

00:01:56.249 --> 00:01:58.217
because later
we talked about it

00:01:58.217 --> 00:02:01.287
and it was like I had this
feeling like if I ever said,

00:02:01.287 --> 00:02:03.322
Hey, we should think
about a speech,

00:02:03.322 --> 00:02:05.391
I would just flash forward
to being like

00:02:05.391 --> 00:02:07.427
50 years later
in a bar with him going,

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:09.128
"Can you believe
we practiced a speech?"

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:11.197
-Yeah, yeah.
"That's why we lost."

00:02:11.197 --> 00:02:14.233
-"Like we would ever win
an Oscar," you know what I mean?

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:15.802
And then suddenly
we were up there

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:19.472
and I just pushed him
into the microphone.

00:02:19.472 --> 00:02:22.041
So he -- and he --
and he just started

00:02:22.041 --> 00:02:23.642
kind of babbling and -- and --

00:02:23.642 --> 00:02:26.713
and then saying names
of people who had helped us.

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:29.882
And then I was next to him and I
was just screaming the names.

00:02:29.882 --> 00:02:32.484
So he would say,
like, "Chris Moore."

00:02:32.484 --> 00:02:35.154
And I'd go, "Chris Moore!"

00:02:35.154 --> 00:02:37.056
And he'd go, "John Gordon,"

00:02:37.056 --> 00:02:40.025
I'd go, "John Gordon!"
-"Gordon! Gordy! Gordy?"

00:02:40.025 --> 00:02:41.894
-I was just so happy.
And like --

00:02:41.894 --> 00:02:44.596
But that was -- that was
my contribution to the speech.

00:02:44.596 --> 00:02:46.199
-Isn't that fun?
-I was like,

00:02:46.199 --> 00:02:48.133
the hype man
in the corner, you know?

00:02:48.133 --> 00:02:50.370
-Do you still have that passion

00:02:50.370 --> 00:02:53.272
and that love of the movies
and the industry?

00:02:53.272 --> 00:02:54.740
-Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.442
I mean, well, that's one of the
reasons we started this company.

00:02:56.442 --> 00:02:58.978
Ben and I started this kind
of mini studio together,

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:01.180
and this was our first movie
for the studio,

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:02.648
which was really cool.

00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:04.249
-What's it called again?

00:03:04.249 --> 00:03:05.418
-Artists Equity.
-Artists Equity.

00:03:05.418 --> 00:03:06.886
-Yeah, yeah.
Because we're pulling a lot of

00:03:06.886 --> 00:03:08.754
the crew members, we're trying
to pull them above the line

00:03:08.754 --> 00:03:10.489
and get them paid better.

00:03:10.489 --> 00:03:12.892
And, you know,
we kind of feel like --

00:03:12.892 --> 00:03:14.360
[ Cheering and applause ]
Yeah, yeah.

00:03:14.360 --> 00:03:17.664
-Good for you.
I read about that.

00:03:17.664 --> 00:03:20.166
-But it's definitely like
a white knuckle exercise

00:03:20.166 --> 00:03:22.535
because we're responsible
for the overages, you know?

00:03:22.535 --> 00:03:26.606
So it's like --
-Did you go over budget?

00:03:26.606 --> 00:03:28.073
-In three places
we went over budget.

00:03:28.073 --> 00:03:30.610
We went over budget,
but in all really good places.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.611
We went over budget
in construction

00:03:32.611 --> 00:03:34.446
because we were moving so fast

00:03:34.446 --> 00:03:36.149
that we had
the construction teams

00:03:36.149 --> 00:03:38.184
working 24 hours a day
to build the sets

00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:40.519
because or else we were going to
run out of stuff to shoot.

00:03:40.519 --> 00:03:44.090
So we had budgeted 33 days
and we shot this in 24.

00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:48.093
So we were moving really fast
without even trying to.

00:03:48.093 --> 00:03:51.230
And then we went over
with our -- we had budgeted,

00:03:51.230 --> 00:03:53.266
we had allotted a certain
amount of money for cast

00:03:53.266 --> 00:03:55.701
and then we got this cast,
so we paid more.

00:03:55.701 --> 00:03:57.337
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:03:57.337 --> 00:03:58.971
-And we were very happy
to pay more.

00:03:58.971 --> 00:04:03.276
And then we went over
budget on food trucks.

00:04:03.276 --> 00:04:05.444
Because we had food trucks.

00:04:05.444 --> 00:04:07.413
-You're a smart guy.
You know how to throw a party.

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:09.414
-Well, we've been doing this
for a long time,

00:04:09.414 --> 00:04:10.683
and we know what matters.

00:04:10.683 --> 00:04:12.018
People were
coming out and, like,

00:04:12.018 --> 00:04:13.686
"There's a Korean
barbecue truck."

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:15.554
-Yeah, that's
exactly right, dude.

00:04:15.554 --> 00:04:17.991
It makes everyone happy.
-Yeah, it was really, really --

00:04:17.991 --> 00:04:21.160
It was the most fun either of us
have ever had making a movie.

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.761
And we've had a lot of fun
making movies.

00:04:22.761 --> 00:04:25.365
-I said it before,
but I really mean it.

00:04:25.365 --> 00:04:27.633
You're one of the best actors
to do it,

00:04:27.633 --> 00:04:31.937
and I love watching you.
It's amazing. You're great.

00:04:31.937 --> 00:04:35.041
You're -- I think that you've,
for me,

00:04:35.041 --> 00:04:37.576
like, if it was a team,
you're my go to player.

00:04:37.576 --> 00:04:39.912
If you're on my team, I go,
just give the ball to Matt.

00:04:39.912 --> 00:04:41.614
He'll nail the line.
You go,

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.916
"How do you like them
apples," or, you know,

00:04:43.916 --> 00:04:47.053
"Ford vs Ferrari," or like,
you know, this stopwatch thing.

00:04:47.053 --> 00:04:48.787
You're like, "You want one?
They're Italian."

00:04:48.787 --> 00:04:52.224
It's like, yes! You have the way
of delivering these lines

00:04:52.224 --> 00:04:54.560
that the crowd goes nuts.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.095
When I watched "Air,"
you had a couple of them.

00:04:56.095 --> 00:04:57.563
Everyone just claps.

00:04:57.563 --> 00:04:59.065
Like, do you go
into a script going,

00:04:59.065 --> 00:05:01.367 size:88% position:56%
"Oh, that line will be my --
that'll be the line I nail"?

00:05:01.367 --> 00:05:02.734
-No, no, no.

00:05:02.734 --> 00:05:05.170
-You just read it
and just do it.

00:05:05.170 --> 00:05:07.639
-Yeah, yeah.
And I mean, you know,

00:05:07.639 --> 00:05:09.441
I look at the whole script

00:05:09.441 --> 00:05:10.810
and usually
if it's a good script,

00:05:10.810 --> 00:05:12.311
then it'll have lines in it.

00:05:12.311 --> 00:05:14.046
But I won't kind of plan
it out like that.

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:16.015
I mean, "how do you like them
apples" was kind of a very --

00:05:16.015 --> 00:05:19.018
because I'm slamming the number
against the window and all that.

00:05:19.018 --> 00:05:21.354
So that definitely felt
like a bigger thing

00:05:21.354 --> 00:05:22.822
because it's a bigger thing
in the movie.

00:05:22.822 --> 00:05:26.091
But not -- I didn't think it
would, like, sustain, you know.

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:28.093
-Is that the line
that everyone yells to you

00:05:28.093 --> 00:05:29.695
when they see you?
-That,

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:32.431
or they just say my name

00:05:32.431 --> 00:05:34.867
like the puppet in
"Team America."

00:05:34.867 --> 00:05:36.536
-Yeah, that's the other way
to do it too.

00:05:36.536 --> 00:05:38.738
-That's the other one
I hear a lot. Yeah.

00:05:38.738 --> 00:05:40.906
-Well, when you --
when you get this idea,

00:05:40.906 --> 00:05:43.175
you go,
okay, this is a fantastic idea.

00:05:43.175 --> 00:05:45.911
This is how kind of the industry
changed, you know?

00:05:45.911 --> 00:05:49.214
And a sneaker company
started working with one person.

00:05:49.214 --> 00:05:51.250
You created
the Air Jordan brand.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.886
You go, okay, first steps.

00:05:53.886 --> 00:05:55.354
We have to talk
to Michael Jordan.

00:05:55.354 --> 00:05:56.456
-Yeah.
-I'm assuming.

00:05:56.456 --> 00:05:57.790
-Yeah, that was the first thing.

00:05:57.790 --> 00:05:59.425
Well, because the script was
great --

00:05:59.425 --> 00:06:00.993
-Can't make it without him.
-Well --

00:06:00.993 --> 00:06:02.061
Well,
he's not in the movie.

00:06:02.061 --> 00:06:04.030
You do realize
he's not in the movie.

00:06:04.030 --> 00:06:06.732
And that's all by design because
it's really about --

00:06:06.732 --> 00:06:09.601
it's about that deal to make
this shoe

00:06:09.601 --> 00:06:12.238
and going back to 1984

00:06:12.238 --> 00:06:14.673
and Nike was not Nike
like the Nike we know.

00:06:14.673 --> 00:06:15.942
They were like this underdog.

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:18.110
They were in third place
in the basketball division.

00:06:18.110 --> 00:06:19.645
-That was, like, already
blew me away.

00:06:19.645 --> 00:06:22.815
When that came up, I'm like,
"Oh, yeah, it was Converse" --

00:06:22.815 --> 00:06:25.484
-Converse and Adidas,
-Then Adidas and then Nike.

00:06:25.484 --> 00:06:28.187
-Yeah. And I'd -- I mean,
I didn't -- I read the script

00:06:28.187 --> 00:06:29.355
and I was like, blown away.

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:31.590
I was like, this is all true?
-Yeah&gt;

00:06:31.590 --> 00:06:34.493
-So that's I think, you know,
Alex, the screenwriter,

00:06:34.493 --> 00:06:37.029
did a really good job
of kind of setting

00:06:37.029 --> 00:06:39.665
what things looked like in 1984.

00:06:39.665 --> 00:06:42.334
And -- and it just was
really surprising.

00:06:42.334 --> 00:06:44.437
-And did you have a meeting
with Michael

00:06:44.437 --> 00:06:46.705
or did you make Ben do that?
-I made Ben do that.

00:06:46.705 --> 00:06:49.508
-Yes. Yeah, yeah, yeah.
"Ben, come on. Charm him."

00:06:49.508 --> 00:06:51.077
-No, Ben went down
to talk to him,

00:06:51.077 --> 00:06:55.214
and actually, that was the kind
of the critical thing,

00:06:55.214 --> 00:06:57.750
because we -- out of that
meeting, Michael --

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.117
Michael --
Because, first of all,

00:06:59.117 --> 00:07:00.686
we said, look,
look, we're not going to do it.

00:07:00.686 --> 00:07:02.521
If you don't want us to do it,
we're not going to do it.

00:07:02.521 --> 00:07:05.424
And he was like, No, no,
it's a good idea for a movie.

00:07:05.424 --> 00:07:07.360
But Ben said, what is --
what's most important

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:08.494
that we get right?

00:07:08.494 --> 00:07:09.662
Like, what's most
important to you?

00:07:09.662 --> 00:07:11.263
And what he spoke about
were three things.

00:07:11.263 --> 00:07:13.298
There was Howard White,

00:07:13.298 --> 00:07:15.734
who's an executive that
Chris Tucker plays in the movie.

00:07:15.734 --> 00:07:17.236
-Oh, he was great!
-Yeah, he's awesome.

00:07:17.236 --> 00:07:21.674
And George Raveling,
who Marlon Wayans plays.

00:07:21.674 --> 00:07:22.941
George Raveling
was the first person

00:07:22.941 --> 00:07:25.311
who talked to Michael
about Nike.

00:07:25.311 --> 00:07:28.947
And then he talked at length
about his mother.

00:07:28.947 --> 00:07:31.650
And that was kind of
the critical thing.

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:33.585
It was like, oh, wait a minute,

00:07:33.585 --> 00:07:35.154
You know, you know,
to see like Michael,

00:07:35.154 --> 00:07:37.756
who's very intimidating dude,

00:07:37.756 --> 00:07:40.259
you know, suddenly kind of
be awestruck

00:07:40.259 --> 00:07:41.426
and talk about his mom.

00:07:41.426 --> 00:07:45.097
And so that was --
so when Ben said like,

00:07:45.097 --> 00:07:47.433
"Look, who do you think
we should get to play" --

00:07:47.433 --> 00:07:50.069
and before he finished
the sentence, the question,

00:07:50.069 --> 00:07:51.803
Michael was like,
"Viola Davis."

00:07:51.803 --> 00:07:52.905
-Yeah.
-So, Ben --

00:07:52.905 --> 00:07:54.840
[ Cheering and applause ]
-Yeah, yeah.

00:07:54.840 --> 00:07:56.475
-I mean, Viola Davis.

00:07:56.475 --> 00:07:58.344
-That's who you go to,
to hit the shot, by the way.

00:07:58.344 --> 00:07:59.444
-Yeah, that's who you go to.

00:07:59.444 --> 00:08:01.247
Yeah, you give it
to Viola Davis.

00:08:01.247 --> 00:08:03.949
-So he called me. He was like,
"I got great news and bad news."

00:08:03.949 --> 00:08:05.350
And I'm like, okay.
And he's like,

00:08:05.350 --> 00:08:08.688
The great news is I know exactly
what to do for the rewrite.

00:08:08.688 --> 00:08:10.355
Like, we got, we're going to put
in George Raveling,

00:08:10.355 --> 00:08:11.557
we're going to
put in Howard White,

00:08:11.557 --> 00:08:12.625
and we're going to make --

00:08:12.625 --> 00:08:16.295
make his mom central to
the whole thing.

00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:17.696
And I go, "Great,
what's the bad news?"

00:08:17.696 --> 00:08:19.232
He goes, "We have to get
Viola Davis

00:08:19.232 --> 00:08:20.433
or we can't make the movie."

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:21.667
-You're like,
All right, all right.

00:08:21.667 --> 00:08:23.536
That is -- because
everyone wants Viola Davis.

00:08:23.536 --> 00:08:25.638
-That's what I said to Ben.
I was like, that's like saying

00:08:25.638 --> 00:08:27.506
you want Michael Jordan
on your basketball team.

00:08:27.506 --> 00:08:29.675
-Exactly.
It's a good way to put it. Yeah.

00:08:29.675 --> 00:08:33.412
-So we went and we rewrote
and we -- and we really

00:08:33.412 --> 00:08:37.449
just kind of drilled down
on that -- on that character

00:08:37.449 --> 00:08:39.952
and the dynamic between her
and everybody else in the movie.

00:08:39.952 --> 00:08:42.588
-She was phenomenal.
-She's phenomenal.

00:08:42.588 --> 00:08:45.358
-She's great.
And it's so well done,

00:08:45.358 --> 00:08:47.960
well acted, well written.
Everything was good.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:50.396
It's funny as well, by the way.
-Yeah.

00:08:50.396 --> 00:08:52.831
It doesn't seem like something
that would be a great movie.

00:08:52.831 --> 00:08:54.933
It's like, it's about
a shoe deal, like.

00:08:54.933 --> 00:08:58.136
But trust me,
it's really -- it's great.

00:08:58.136 --> 00:09:00.005
-You're cheering --
-It's a lot of fun.

00:09:00.005 --> 00:09:02.141
-By the end -- oh!
No, it makes you feel good.

00:09:02.141 --> 00:09:03.876
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:09:03.876 --> 00:09:05.378
That's what we used
to call "a feel good movie."

00:09:05.378 --> 00:09:07.312
-Feel good movie of the year.
Yeah.

00:09:07.312 --> 00:09:10.182
And it's like at the end,
you're like, standing ovation

00:09:10.182 --> 00:09:12.551
and clapping, like, "Yes,
I'm so happy for everybody!"

00:09:12.551 --> 00:09:15.187
It's wild. You're getting
fantastic reviews.

00:09:15.187 --> 00:09:17.523
A lot of critics are saying this
is the best movie of the year

00:09:17.523 --> 00:09:18.824
so far,
so congratulations.

00:09:18.824 --> 00:09:21.627
